const makePost = (slug: string, title: string, excerpt: string, focus: string, workflow: string, boundary: string, checks: string, example: string): NewPost => {
  const paragraphs = [
    `Start with the actual ${focus} queue rather than an abstract job title. The firm should identify where each item arrives, which matter or client identifier travels with it, what a finished preparation looks like, and which person owns the next decision. For offshore legal support, the point is not to move responsibility out of the firm. It is to make repeatable preparation visible, consistent, and easy for a lawyer or manager to review. Before access is granted, collect two or three approved examples, mark sensitive fields, and write down the stop condition. If the source is missing, conflicting, or unexpectedly urgent, the worker records the gap and pauses. That small discipline prevents a neat-looking output from hiding an unexamined assumption.`,
    `A workable ${workflow} has a clear intake, preparation, review, and closeout path. At intake, record the requestor, matter, source location, received time, requested result, and owner. During preparation, preserve the source, use the current firm-approved format, and distinguish copied facts from notes or questions. The reviewer should be able to compare the output with the source without asking the worker to reconstruct what happened. Review is not a ceremonial sign-off: it is where the firm checks substance, confidentiality, client promises, and any unusual circumstance. At closeout, record the disposition, unresolved items, rework reason, and next owner. A queue is healthy when another person can understand its state without relying on private chat history.`,
    `The role boundary matters most when ${boundary}. Offshore support may gather, label, sort, format, reconcile against an approved checklist, and report what is absent. It should not decide legal meaning, privilege, strategy, eligibility, liability, filing sufficiency, settlement position, or what advice a client should receive. The worker also should not silently repair a contradictory record by choosing the version that seems most plausible. A better response is to preserve both sources, describe the conflict neutrally, and route one precise question to the named reviewer. The firm retains supervision, client communication, legal judgment, and final release. This division makes the role safer and also improves training because the worker knows what a complete handoff contains.`,
    `For quality control, measure ${checks} at the level of individual work, not only at the level of volume. A manager can sample whether the right source was used, whether identifiers and dates were retained, whether missing information was marked, whether permissions matched the task, and whether escalation happened promptly. Track rework by reason: omitted field, wrong source, formatting defect, unclear instruction, or reviewer change. Those categories tell the firm whether to coach the person, repair the process, or narrow the scope. Review early work daily, then change the cadence only after the same task type is consistently understandable and exceptions reach the right owner. Never turn a low error count into a claim that legal risk has disappeared; the scorecard is a control signal, not a legal conclusion.`,
    `Consider ${example}. The worker receives an approved request and two source files, but one carries a different date and the second has an incomplete identifier. The correct output is not a confident merged record. It is a prepared item that links both files, states exactly where they differ, names the missing field, and sends the question to the firm contact. If the reviewer confirms which source controls, that instruction becomes part of the record and can improve the next example. If the request changes scope, reaches a new recipient, or requires a legal conclusion, the worker stops even when the queue is busy. Concrete examples like this are more useful than broad promises because they show how the role behaves under ordinary ambiguity.`,
    `The operating setup should also account for confidentiality. Use named accounts, least-access permissions, matter-specific folders where practical, and a documented route for a suspected wrong-file or wrong-recipient event. Training examples should be redacted when the task can be learned without live client information. Keep source and working copies distinct, avoid downloading material that the task does not require, and remove access when the assignment changes. The firm should decide retention, monitoring, incident response, and contractual requirements with appropriate professional advice. Offshore location is not a substitute for those controls, and a control is not real merely because it appears in a policy. The manager should be able to show who reviews the work and how an exception becomes visible.`,
    `A sensible pilot adds one lane at a time. Write the role brief in the language of outputs: what arrives, what is prepared, what evidence accompanies it, who reviews it, and what must never be released without approval. Test the instructions against a clean example and an intentionally incomplete example. During the first week, review every output and keep a short question log. At the end of the pilot, decide whether the task is ready to continue, needs a revised checklist, or should remain with the firm. Adding a second lane is justified by stable quality and review capacity, not by a desire to fill idle time. For LegalServicesOffshore.com readers, this is the practical value of offshore legal support: a disciplined operating lane that helps a legal team work through its queue while the firm keeps the decisions that matter. The manager should also compare the written instructions with the real queue at the end of the pilot. If workers repeatedly ask about the same field, recipient, source, or exception, that pattern is evidence that the process needs clarification. Update the approved example and stop rule through the firm’s normal owner, then review a fresh sample. This keeps improvement tied to observed work rather than assumptions about what a remote role ought to do.`
  ];
  const headings = ['Define the work before access', 'Use a traceable handoff', 'Keep legal judgment with the firm', 'Check quality and exceptions', 'Work through an ambiguous example', 'Protect the matter record', 'Pilot, review, and expand carefully'];
  const sourceSegment = august18RouteSourceSegments[slug];
  return { slug, title, excerpt, minutes: 9, published: sourceSegment.published, sourceDate: sourceSegment.sourceDate, sections: headings.map((heading, i) => ({ heading, body: paragraphs[i] })) };
};

